# Copy to .env.docker for `docker compose up`. Every value is optional — the
# stack boots on dev-safe placeholders without this file — but anything that
# must be a real secret, or that differs from the defaults baked into
# deploy/docker-entrypoint.sh, belongs here.
#
# The api container turns these into per-service services/<name>/.env files on
# first boot. A mounted real .env always wins; the entrypoint never overwrites
# one that already exists.
#
# Third-party keys (Stripe, DeepL, Lexware, the GitHub rebuild dispatch) are
# deliberately NOT here. They are set at runtime in the admin panel under
# Einstellungen and stored encrypted in `app_setting`, so that rotating one is
# not a deploy. See project docs: runtime settings store.

# --- database (one MariaDB, one schema per service) ------------------------
DB_USER=tds
DB_PASS=tds
DB_ROOT_PASS=root

# --- ports on the host -----------------------------------------------------
# The gateway container always listens on 8000 internally.
API_PORT=8080
# The `frontends` profile (see docker-compose.yml).
LANDING_PORT=4321
BLOG_PORT=4322
ADMIN_PORT=4323
CUSTOMER_PORT=4324
TOOLS_PORT=4325
AUTH_PORT=4326

# --- shared across services ------------------------------------------------
# The admin Bearer, shared by auth + customer. Not a login: the shared-token
# paste-login is gone, real admins are app_user rows (see create-admin below).
ADMIN_TOKEN=dev-admin-token-change-me

# How the frontend + customer services reach auth for JWKS verification.
# Inside the container everything is one process on :8000, hence the loopback.
AUTH_API_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8080/auth

# Every browser origin allowed to call the API with credentials. This must name
# each origin EXACTLY — `*` is forbidden together with credentials, and a
# response the browser rejects for this reason produces no server-side error at
# all. The list below matches the six ports above.
CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=http://localhost:4321,http://localhost:4322,http://localhost:4323,http://localhost:4324,http://localhost:4325,http://localhost:4326

# --- auth ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Empty JWT_PRIVATE_KEY → the entrypoint generates a dev RS256 keypair once.
JWT_PRIVATE_KEY=
JWT_KEY_ID=tds-auth-dev-1
JWT_ISSUER=http://127.0.0.1:8080/auth
# Cookies ignore ports, so one `localhost` cookie is shared by every frontend
# port above — which is what makes cross-panel SSO work locally.
COOKIE_DOMAIN=localhost

# --- frontend + customer ---------------------------------------------------
# Encrypts the runtime secrets stored in `app_setting` (AES-256-GCM). Lose it
# and the stored values are unreadable — there is no recovery path.
SETTINGS_ENCRYPTION_KEY=dev-settings-encryption-key-change-me

# Signs document download links (customer + the documents extension).
DOCUMENT_SIGN_SECRET=dev-document-sign-secret-change-me

# SMTP for the frontend mailer. Empty → the NullMailer: mail is composed and
# discarded, which is usually what you want locally. QUOTE any value with a
# space in it; phpdotenv rejects a bare spaced value and Bootstrap loads the
# .env before anything else, so one such line takes the whole service down.
MAIL_DSN=

# --- optional --------------------------------------------------------------
# GATEWAY_MODE=proxy runs each backend as its own loopback php -S process
# instead of in-process. Only for debugging; set it HERE and nowhere else —
# see the note in docker-compose.yml about the project's own ./.env.
# GATEWAY_MODE=proxy

# Site keys and page-cache tokens for the three public sites do NOT belong
# here: this file is the API container's env_file, and the frontend services
# read theirs from ./.env.frontends (see .env.frontends.example). The split is
# not cosmetic — Compose interpolates `${…}` out of the project's ./.env, so a
# value put here for a frontend would have become an empty string in silence.
